# gjs -- GNOME's JavaScript bindings, and the last package in this chain.
#
# gnome-shell and gnome-settings-daemon are GJS applications: the shell is
# JavaScript from its top-level down, and without this there is no session to
# start. Nothing in the tree provided it, which is why this chain exists.
#
# THE PATCH IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT A LOCAL FIX. gjs 1.84.2's meson.build
# reads `spidermonkey = dependency('mozjs-128')`; BLFS 12.4 ships this exact
# version against SpiderMonkey 140 and carries
# gjs-1.84.2-spidermonkey_140-1.patch to do it. That patch is eleven UPSTREAM
# commits (the pkg-config name, plus the API adaptations for
# JS::EnvironmentChain, JS::Heap::address and the rest), collected by BLFS with
# "Upstream Status: Applied". It is in patches/ as 0001-blfs-spidermonkey-140.patch.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gjs/1.84/gjs-1.84.2.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=35142edf345705636300291ec3a7d583f14969ff3fae0ff30f4a95b1e6740166
SRC_DIR=gjs-1.84.2

# -Dinstalled_tests=false. THIS ONE DEFAULTS TO TRUE, which is the trap json-glib
# already sprang here: leaving it alone ships gjs's test programs and their data
# into /usr/libexec/installed-tests and /usr/share/installed-tests on every
# machine. The option is passed rather than merely noted, because the default is
# the wrong way round.
#
# -Dreadline=enabled, NOT auto, and this is the most consequential line here.
# gjs does not use pkg-config for readline: it calls cxx.find_library('readline')
# and then COMPILES AND RUNS a program that calls readline("foo"). If that link
# fails -- which it does when libreadline has no terminal library behind it --
# then under `auto` the whole feature is silently dropped and the result is a gjs
# whose interactive shell and debugger cannot read a line. Under `enabled` it is
# an error. See pkgs/readline/install.sh, which asserts the DT_NEEDED entry that
# makes this probe link.
#
# -Dprofiler=disabled. 'auto' with a subproject fallback on sysprof, which is
# not packaged. _scripts/build-meson.sh passes --wrap-mode=nodownload, so a
# fallback here can never quietly become a vendored copy -- but naming the
# option means the answer does not change the day something else brings sysprof
# into the seed.
#
# -Dskip_dbus_tests=true and -Dskip_gtk_tests=true. Both are about what the
# BUILD requires, not about what is tested: dbus-run-session is a find_program
# whose `required:` is literally `not get_option('skip_dbus_tests')`, and the
# gtk test block wants a display connection that a build container does not
# have. Neither affects the shipped library.
#
# NOT PASSED, and each was checked rather than assumed:
#   -Ddtrace / -Dsystemtap        already default false
#   -Dbsymbolic_functions         already default true
#   -Dverbose_logs                already default false, and meson.build calls
#                                 error() if it is true on a release build
#   -Dcpp_rtti                    a meson builtin, left at its default
MESON_ARGS="-Dinstalled_tests=false -Dreadline=enabled -Dprofiler=disabled \
-Dskip_dbus_tests=true -Dskip_gtk_tests=true"
